export const PLANT_PROFILES: PlantProfile[] = [
  {
    id: 'kalo',
    name: 'Kalo',
    scientific: 'Colocasia esculenta',
    accent: '#3d7a4a',
    otherNames: `Taro, Talo, Dalo, Abalong, Imo, Cocoyam`,
    uses: `Canoe plant, staple food, steamed and mashed, center of mythology/spiritualism/social structure.`,
    kinolau: `Kāne`,
    profile: `Believed to originate in Asia. Corms are high in carbohydrates and contain calcium oxalate crystals that must be heated before eating. Leaves are rich in vitamin A/C and also contain calcium, iron, thiamine, and riboflavin. Propagated by cloning. Reaches maturity after 7-11 months. Corms average about 2-5lbs in weight. Different cultivars produce different qualities and characteristics. Production impacted by method of cultivation, weather conditions, water conditions, etc.`,
    vocab: [`Lau/ lūʻau (leaf)`, `Hā (petiole)`, `Huli (the part of the stalk used for propagating)`, `Kalo (corm)`, `ʻOhā (smaller corm/offshoot growing off of main corm)`],
    other: [`In Hawaiʻi, "kalo" generally refers to Colocasia esculenta, but can also refer to the Xanthosoma, Alocasia, and Cyrtosperma genera. Taro root aphids are difficult to see due to their size but can be identified by the waxy white fluff that covers them. Infested huli can be treated before planting by soaking in a bath of 120-degree water for 6 minutes, then placing the huli in cool water. Apple snails will feed on the entire taro from leaf to corm and create openings for disease and other pests to enter the plant. Apple snails thrive in flooded fields and fresh water sources, so a dry-down period can help to clear an infested patch. Ducks are good for snail-control in flooded loʻi. Wear gloves when removing snail eggs by hand (bunches of small pink eggs on the petiole indicate apple snails). Vinegar can be used to relieve "taro itch" caused by calcium oxalate crystals if they come into contact with skin. A rinse of cold water, salt, and baking soda can also help with skin or mouth irritation. Use gloves when handling and cutting corms to prevent "taro itch." Names for individual cultivars varied from island to island, a single cultivar may have multiple names. Lūʻau is at peak production in summer and can be harvested every 2-3 weeks. During winter, harvesting can be done every 3-4 weeks. Removing leaves from the plant will slow development of the corm. Older plants produce tougher leaves that take longer to cook. Common diseases are leaf blight, soft rot (pala/ palahi), southern blight, leaf spot, pocket rot, dry rot, black rot, soft rot, dasheen mosaic, and loliloli.`, `Bishop Museum's Hawaiʻi Biological Survey site ([www.hbs.bishopmuseum.org/botany/taro](http://www.hbs.bishopmuseum.org/botany/taro)) offers a guide identifying cultivars`, `CTAHR website (<https://www.ctahr.hawaii.edu/site/Info.aspx>) has resources for:<br>• taro varieties and their characteristics<br>• identifying and managing pests and diseases<br>• approved herbicides and pesticides<br>• appropriate cover crops<br>• propagation via tissue culture`],
    planting: `Can be planted year-round in Hawaiʻi, but is best planted between April and September. Can be planted in multiple ways including loʻi, ridges/furrows, terraces, mounds, holes, etc. Kalo is typically planted in rows spaced roughly three feet apart. Plant in spaces with at least 10" of soil. Areas with good air circulation and day-long sunshine are preferred. New plots should be rested for at least one month before planting because decomposing green plant material will compete for Nitrogen with newly planted kalo (as will immature compost). Reused plots should be allowed to lie fallow for at least a few months, but up to a year if disease or pests are present. "Dryland" and "wetland" varieties should be used in appropriate settings. Fern fronds were traditionally used to protect dryland kalo beds during times of intense sunlight, and removed during periods of rain. Fertilize only for the first 6 months of growth. During the first 4 months of growth, weeds may outcompete taro for soil nutrients, so weed 2-3 times a month until taro leaves create a shade canopy. About 2" of course mulch at the base of the plant will also help suppress weed growth. Planting moons are Hilo, Hoaka, Kū Kahi, Kū Lua, Kū Kolu, Kū Pau, Mōhalu, Akua, Hoku, Māhealani, Kāne, Lono, and Mauli. Kalo plants used for their leaves were traditionally planted on Hilo and Hoaka moons. Kalo plants used for their corms were traditionally planted on Mōhalu and Hua moons.`,
    mythologyRefs: [`*Kalo Traditions and the Ancestry of the Hawaiians* (Lāʻau Hawaiʻi: Traditional Hawaiian Uses of Plants, pp.23)`, `*The Origins of Kalo in Hawaiʻi* (Taro Mauka to Makai, pp. 17-18)`, `*Food for Kohala* (Hawaiʻi Island Legends, pp.205-209)`],
    textExcerpts: [`"Taro is indisputably the most important plant in Hawaiian culture. Besides being the staple food source, it was also the center of spiritualism, mythology, and social structure. In Hawaiian lore, the *kalo* plant is literally the elder brother of mankind. As the elder brother, *kalo* cares for and nourishes mankind, and we, in return, must respect and care for *kalo*. The *kalo* plant is high in carbohydrates, vitamin A, B, and C, and a variety of other elements and enzymes that are important to healthy living. *Kalo* contains calcium oxalate crystals in the leaves and tubers, and traditionally had to be steamed for over an hour before the crystals broke down and the plant became edible. Steamed *kalo* was then mashed into *poi* which was the staple food of old Hawaiʻi. Breakfast, lunch, and dinner usually centered on *poi*, and the signal to begin eating was the uncovering of the *poi* bowl. *Kalo* is typically cloned, with each progressive crop being derived from the last harvest." (Lincoln 2009 p. 84-85)`, `"Kalo and 'uala were the most important crops for the ancient Hawaiians. They furnished not only the carbohydrate base of their diet and goodly amounts of high-quality vitamins and minerals but also were *kinolau* of the gods Kāne and Lono, respectively." (Abbott 1992 p. 23)`, `"The myth of the origin of the Hawaiian people is known in several versions, but all of them begin similarly and give kalo a prominent place. When the sky principle Wākea coupled with the earth principle Papa, their first child, Hāloanaka, was stillborn, but a kalo plant sprouted from the place where the fetus was buried. Their second child was a daughter, Hoʻohōkūkalani, by whom Wākea later had a human son named Hāloa, the ancestor of the Hawaiian aliʻi and thus of all Hawaiians." (Abbott 1992 p. 23)`, `"Taro is full of needle-like and knob-like crystals of calcium oxalate that make it very painful to eat raw but which can be dissolved and rendered harmless by exposure to heat." (Abbott 1992 p. 25)`, `"Since kalo rarely produces seeds, it is always propagated in one of three other ways. The most common is to [use] huli from the upper six to eight centimeters (two or three inches) of the corm, containing the stem apex, and the lower ten to twelve centimeters (four to five inches) of the hā. The alternative methods are to plant buds borne in the axils of the leaves or to plant ʻohā, which are outgrowths produced by adventitious roots of the parent plant." (Abbott 1992 p. 26)`, `"In experiments with eight varieties of kalo, all maturing in six to twelve months, Dr. Handy found only one that needed to be harvested promptly at maturity. The other varieties could remain in the flooded loʻi for two to twenty-four months after reaching maturity. This feature of the plant makes kalo a very flexible crop and allowed loʻi to double as a storehouse. The planter could harvest what he needed, perhaps a little at a time." (Abbott 1992 p. 26)`, `"Dryland varieties take a little more time to mature than wetland kalo---eight to twelve months. Depending on the variety, they may remain in the soil three to twelve months beyond maturity, or even longer." (Abbott 1992 p. 27)`, `"All cultivars (cultivated varieties) of taro can be grown under non-flooded conditions, but not all can grow well in flooded soil. Ancient Hawaiians divided taro into wetland and upland types based on the place(s) and the cultivation system that best suited each cultivar." (Evans 2008 p. 29)`, `"Ancient Hawaiians were descriptive in naming the different cultivars of taro, and they often grouped them into families based on the plants' characteristics. Within large families, members were given a 'first' and 'last' name, similar to how a scientific botanical name is given; the first indicating the group, such as Mana, Lauloa, or Piko, that the cultivar belongs to (similar to a genus name) and the second describing a quality distinguishing the cultivar from the others, such as the fairest or the darkest, or the shortest or the tallest (in the same manner that a species name is given)." (Evans 2008 p. 34)`, `"[The] Lehua [family of taro] is known for its beautiful lilac-purple poi. Lehuas are also known as the 'royal taros' and were formerly grown for and eaten only by the aliʻi. This is the main type grown in Hanalei on Kauaʻi, with over 300 acres in production. High-yielding and early-maturing, Lehua taros are ready to harvest in 8-12 months, depending on the cultivar. A downside is they cannot be held in the patch once mature, because of their tendency to rot... [Lehua] are used for both flooded and non-flooded culture. Lehua ʻEleʻele is one of the fastest maturing of the Lehua family, at 7-8 months in some non-flooded situations." (Evans 2008 p.35)`, `"Mana means 'branching' and refers to branching corms, a characteristic peculiar to [the Mana] family [of taro]. These taros have a rubbery consistency when made into kūlolo, and they make poi of a fair quality and are excellent as table taro. This is one of the most drought-tolerant families. Grown primarily in non-flooded culture... The leaves are good for lūʻau. Mana ʻUlu makes yellow poi and is a favorite on Molokaʻi for making kūlolo and for table taro. It is one of the fastest maturing cultivars, at 7-8 months, similar to Lehua ʻEleʻele. Mana Keʻokeʻo produces large corms and is one of the best for making kūlolo." (Evans 2008 p.35-36)`, `"Piko taros have a characteristic leaf form in which the sinus (the "V" of the leaf blade) extends down to the petiole; thus they are easy to identify... These taros are the most late-maturing, averaging 12-15 months, and can be held in the patch for extended periods. This is considered one of the hardiest taro families, was the most common for flooded culture, and was the main taro family grown in Hālawa valley on Molokaʻi. Piko cultivars grow best in cooler areas having an abundance of cool water. They have a leathery looking corm skin and are resistant to root rot but are very susceptible to leaf blight. They can also be grown in non-flooded culture." (Evans 2008 p. 36)`, `"Lauloa is a family [of taro] known for its drought tolerance and is preferred for making kūlolo. It is said that of all the Hawaiʻi taros, Lauloa most resembled the legendary Hāloa, because it is tall and overshadows its keiki. Grown exclusively in non-flooded culture, it is also used for medicine... Lauloa Keʻokeʻo has been considered by some to produce the highest quality leaf for lūʻau of all the Hawaiian taro varieties." (Evans 2008 p.36)`, `"ʻEleʻele is a [taro] family grown in non-flooded culture and known for its red poi; these were the royal 'black' taros in ancient Hawaiʻi and were especially popular in the Kona area. The darkest of all Hawaiian taros, this is one of the favorites of growers in Hoʻolehua today. The two main types are ʻEleʻele Naioea and ʻEleʻele Mākoko, although some believe that Lauloa ʻEleʻele ʻUla belongs in this family. ʻEleʻele Naioea makes an excellent purple poi comparable to that from the Lahua family, but ʻEleʻele Mākoko has a higher yield." (Evans 2008 p. 36)`, `"Although most growers prefer to plant freshly harvested material, some choose to give smaller huli a 'jump-start' to increase their chances of survival. This is done either during drought conditions or to avoid weed competition by planting larger huli. It's done by holding huli in buckets or tanks containing a few inches of clean, fresh water for a few weeks. Plant them when they have two or three healthy leaves and a good mass of roots." (Evans 2008 p. 39)`, `"One way to get strong huli is to harvest the entire plant before it begins to 'drop down' or shrink in the later stages of growth. Use the strong juvenile huli from ʻohā as planting material for your next crop. Big, healthy, young huli usually produce big, healthy taro plants. Corms harvested early may be mature but probably are not at their full potential size." (Evans 2008 p. 39)`, `"In Hawaiʻi, taro is planted year-round, but it grows best when planted during spring and summer (April- September). During fall and winter (October-March), solar radiation is approximately half of that received during the warmer part of the year, and the cooler temperatures slow taro growth. Slower growth aids corm maturation and is desirable at the end of the growing cycle, but is not desirable when the plant is just beginning its life. Taro planted during winter has the disadvantage of slow corm growth in the early stages, which increases the incidence of disease at maturity and makes it harder to compete with weeds. Therefore, planting taro during the fall and winter months requires relatively more weeding and monitoring for disease. It is important to keep in mind that planting times and thus harvesting times vary with location and elevation because of differing climates." (Evans 2008 p. 41)`, `"If you plant huli close togehter (both within and between rows), the crop will yield more corm but have fewer ʻohā. Conversely, the further apart you plant huli, the more ʻohā you can expect to produce. However, if plants are too close together it will be difficult to work in the planting. Also, close plants may make it easier for insect pests to move among them, and if sunlight and air circulation is too restricted in the planting, moisture levels on the plants will be high and some diseases can occur more readily. Spacing huli too far apart, however, will make it difficult for the leaves to form a complete weed-suppressing canopy." (Evans 2008 p. 42)`, `"You will know when the taro is ready to be harvested when:<br>• the plants are at least 6 months old (or older, depending on the variety)<br>• most older leaves turn yellow and die off over a short period of time<br>• there are fewer new leaves being produced<br>• the new leaves that do grow are smaller than preceding ones<br>• most of the roots are gone or have shrunk (this will require you to pull some taro up and check for corm maturity)<br>• the top of the corm is above the soil surface and the diameter of the top of the corm is decreasing (forming a "neck").<br>These are all signs of maturity, when the plant's final energy is going into increasing the size of the corm to its maximum." (Evans 2008 p. 52)`],
    interviewTitle: ``,
    interviewLines: [],
    oleloNoeau: [{ num: `83`, text: `ʻAi no i kalo moʻa (One can eat cooked taro). The work is done; one can sit at ease and enjoy himself.` }, { num: `263`, text: `E ʻao lūʻau a kualima (Offer young taro leaves to the gods five times). Advice to one has erred and wishes to rectify his mistake. Young taro leaves oftern were substituted for pigs when making an offering to the gods. To remove sickness of mind or body, one made five separate offerings of young taro leaves.` }, { num: `316`, text: `E kanu i ka huli ʻoi hāʻule ka ua (Plant the taro stalks while there is rain). Do your work when opportunity affords.` }, { num: `329`, text: `E lawe i ke ō, he hinana ka iʻa kuhi lima (Take vegetable food; the hinana is a fish that can be caught in the hand). A suggestion to take taro, poi, potato, or breadfruit along on the journey and not worry about meats, which can be found along the way. First uttered by Pele in a chant about the winds of Kauaʻi.` }, { num: `340`, text: `E! Loaʻa aku la ke kalo, o ka ʻapowale (Say! You'll obtain a taro, the ʻapowale). You are wasting your time. A play on ʻapo-wale (grasp-at-nothing), a variety of taro.` }, { num: `416`, text: `Hakē ka paʻi ʻai o ka Malulani (The Malulani is overloaded with bundles of hard poi). An impolite reference to a pregnant woman. The Malulani was an inter-island ship.` }, { num: `429`, text: `Hālau ka hale; ʻohā ka ʻai (A big house; small taro to eat). A large house brings so many visitors that to feed them all, even immature taro must be used.` }, { num: `666`, text: `He kalo paʻa (Unpounded taro). A spinster or a bachelor.` }, { num: `671`, text: `He manu Mahoemua, he kalo pūʻali (When one plants in [the month of] Mahoemua, he will have irregularly shaped taro).` }, { num: `814`, text: `He mea ʻao lūʻau ʻia ke kānāwai (A law [of an ʻaumakua] can be removed with an offering of cooked taro leaves). An ʻaumakua could be propitiated by offering taro leaves and prayers for forgiveness.` }, { num: `850`, text: `He ʻohā pili wale (A young taro that attaches itself to an older corm). Said of a person who attaches himself to another in order to receive care. He is like a young taro that grows beside the parent plant but does not belong to it.` }, { num: `892`, text: `He pili kauawe paha ke kumu i moʻa ʻole ai ke kalo (Perhaps the reason for the partly cooked condition of the taro is because it is the one closest to the leaves that cover over the imu). Said of an imperfect or defective task, or of a person whose ideas are "half-baked."` }, { num: `997`, text: `Hilo ʻai lūʻau (Hilo, eater of taro greens). The people of Hilo were said to be fond of cooked taro greens. When storms came to Hilo it was impossible to obtain fish from the streams or the sea. The people had to be content with taro greens.` }, { num: `1127`, text: `Hui aku, hui mai, hui kalo me ka nāwao (Mixed there, mixed here, all mixed together are the cultivated and the wild taros). Said of a great mix-up.` }, { num: `1232`, text: `I maikaʻi ke kalo i ka ʻohā (The goodness of the taro is judged by the young plant it produces). Parents are often judged by the behavior of their children.` }, { num: `1346`, text: `Ka iʻa i nui ai o Kamehameha (The fish on which Kamehameha was raised). Taro greens. The Kamehameha mentioned here is the son of Kekaulike, ruler of Maui, not Kamehameha I, the conqueror. Once, when it was necessary for his personal attendants to be gone for the day, the chief, who was then a small child, was left in the care of his attendants' two young sons. Taro greens had been prepared and cooked for the royal child, because they were tender and easy to swallow. Kekaulike arrived unexpectedly and was displeased to see only taro greens instead of fish being given to his son. When the boys, who did not recognize him, explained that this was a very precious child and that the taro greens were fed him because they had no bones that would lodge in his throat, Kekaulike was pleased. Thus the little chief, who was reared at Pakaikai, Molokaʻi, became known as Kamehameha-nui-ʻai-lūʻau (Great Kemehameha, Eater-of-taro-greens).` }, { num: `1360`, text: `Ka iʻa lau nui o ka ʻāina (Big-leaved fish of the land). Lūʻau, or taro greens.` }, { num: `1361`, text: `Ka iʻa lauoho loloa o ka ʻāina (The long-haired fish of the land). Any vegetable eaten with poi, such as taro greens, hoʻiʻo or kikawaiō ferns, or sweet potato greens. Poetically, leaves are the oho or lauoho, hair, of plants.` }, { num: `1447`, text: `Kalo kanu o ka ʻāina (Taro planted on the land). Natives of the land from generations back.` }, { num: `1509`, text: `Kanu ke kalo i Welo, ʻaʻole e ulu nui ʻia e ka ʻohā (Plant taro in Welo and the offshoots will not be many). The corm of taro planted in the month of Welo grows very large but the offshoots are few.` }, { num: `1700`, text: `Ke hōʻole mai nei o Hāloa (Hāloa denies that). Hāloa is the god of taro. It was said that whatever business was discussed before an open poi bowl was denied by Hāloa. If a medical kahuna was called while eating, he took it as a sign that he was not the right person to treat the sick one. However, if he was told while eating that someone was dying, he was able to treat the illness, for Hāloa would deny the death.` }, { num: `1735`, text: `Ke kalo paʻa o Waiahole (The hard taro of Waiahole). A reminder not to treat others badly. One day, a man went to Waiahole, Oʻahu, to visit his sister, whom he had not seen for many years. She was absent, and her husband neither asked the stranger in nor offered him any food. When hunger possessed the visitor he asked if he might have some taro to eat. His brother-in-law directed him to his taro patches and told him to get some from there. The man went to the patches and then continued on his way. When the woman returned she was told of the visitor, and by her husband's description she knew that it was her brother. She rebuked him for his lack of hospitality. When they went to their taro patches they found all the taro pulled up and hacked to pieces.` }, { num: `1953`, text: `Lau-kapili. Lau-kapalala (Tremble-leaf. Broad-leaf). These were the names applied to the leaves of the very first taro plant, which according to legend was named Hāloa. Taro leaves are sometimes referred to poetically by these terms.` }, { num: `1987`, text: `Lele no ka ʻohe i kona lua (The ʻohe taro leaps into its own hole). Each person to his own place. From the legend of Kamiki, in which the hero called to the various taros by name, and each leaped into its own hole and stood there.` }, { num: `2106`, text: `Make no ke kalo a ola i ka naio (The taro dies but lives again in the pinworm). The matter may be thought dead, but it is likely to come alive again. Naio (pinworms) were sometimes found in poi and caused itching in the anal passage.` }, { num: `2107`, text: `Make no ke kalo a ola i ka palili (The taro may die but lives on in the young plants that it produces). One lives on in his children.` }, { num: `2135`, text: `Mānā, i ka puʻe kalo hoʻoneʻeneʻe a ka wai (Mānā, where the mounded taro moves in the water). Refers to Mānā, Kauaʻi. In ancient days there were five patches at Kolo, Mānā, in which deep water mound-planting was done for taro. As the plants grew, the rootless were allowed to spread undisturbed because they helped to hold the soil together. When the rainy season came, the whole area was flooded as far as Kalamaihiki, and it took weeks for the water to subside. The farmers built rafts of sticks and rushes, then dived into the water. They worked the bases of the taro mounds free and lifted them carefully, so as not to disturb the soil, to the rafts where they were secured. The weight of the mounds submerged the rafts but permitted the taro stalks to grow above water just as they did before the flood came. The rafts were tied together to form a large, floating field of taro.` }, { num: `2395`, text: `O ka ʻai no ka ʻai, o ka ʻiʻo kanaka ka iʻa (Food is here to be eaten, with only human flesh for meat). Said when there is nothing to eat with poi. There were once two boys of Kaʻū who won a riddling contest against a Kona man, the champion of the island of Hawaiʻi. In one riddle the boys claimed to be eating human flesh. The audience pondered this, since no meat was visible, and began to dispute the claim. Suddenly the boys popped wads of poi into their mouths and proceeded to lick their fingers---the "human flesh."` }, { num: `2523`, text: `ʻOno kāhi ʻao lūʻau me ke aloha pū (A little taro green is delicious when love is present). Even the plainest of fare is delicious when there is love.` }, { num: `2652`, text: `Pili ʻohā, he kāmau mai mawaho (A taro-offshoot relationship added to the outside of the corm). One who was not a relative, yet is a member of the household.` }, { num: `2705`, text: `Pūʻali kalo i ka wai ʻole (Taro, for lack of water, grows misshapen). For lack of care, one may become ill.` }],
  },
  {
    id: 'uala',
    name: 'ʻUala',
    scientific: 'Ipomoea batatas',
    accent: '#b5651d',
    otherNames: `Sweet potato, Kumara, Batata, Patata`,
    uses: `Canoe plant, food, allowed for greater inland settlement.`,
    kinolau: `Lono`,
    profile: `Originates in South America, thrives in arid conditions, rich in carbohydrates, thiamine, and vitamin A, contains vitamin B1/B2/C, protein, phosphorus, more than 50 Hawaiian varieties, can be harvested after 6-8 months.`,
    vocab: [],
    other: [],
    planting: `Can be planted year-round, but best planted after the end of the rainy season to avoid waterlogging. Vulnerable to drought/waterlogging for the first 40 days after planting. Often planted in mounds. Planting moons are Hilo, Hoaka, Kū Kahi, Kū Lua, Kū Pau, Hūnā, Mōhalu, Akua, Hoku, Māhealani, Kulu, Kāne, Lono, and Mauli.`,
    mythologyRefs: [`*Food for Kohala* (Hawaiʻi Island Legends, pp.205-209)`],
    textExcerpts: [`"Hawaiian farmers removed rocks from their planting areas and arranged them into low, mounded walls called *kuaiwi*, which ran down-slope. Between the *kuaiwi* were planted primary crops such as *kalo* or *ʻuala*, and along the edges of the *kuaiwi* were planted secondary crops, such as banana and sugarcane. It is thought that these taller border crops created shade, captured and preserved moisture, served as windbreaks, and provided mulch for the primary crops." (Lincoln 2009 p. 79)`, `"The sweet potato was an embodiment of Lono, the god of rain and agriculture (among other things). The sweet potato poses a great question because it is the only Polynesian-introduced plant that does not originate in Southeast Asia. In fact, it hails from the opposite side of the Pacific Ocean in South America. There are several competing theories about the sweet potato's arrival in Polynesia... However it arrived in Hawaiʻi, the sweet potato gained a strong importance here. The Hawaiian Islands are the tallest of the Polynesian islands. The semi-desert and desert habitats of the leeward mountain slopes were too dry to support large populations on taro, which required additional water. The sweet potato, however, thrived in these arid conditions and allowed the Hawaiians to increase the size of their dry-land settlements." (Lincoln 2009 p. 94)`, `"Kalo and 'uala were the most important crops for the ancient Hawaiians. They furnished not only the carbohydrate base of their diet and goodly amounts of high-quality vitamins and minerals but also were *kinolau* of the gods Kāne and Lono, respectively." (Abbott 1992 p. 23)`, `"Sweet potato, or ʻuala, ranked second only to kalo in providing carbohydrates and minerals in the Hawaiian diet. ʻUala were cultivated and eaten throughout the islands but were important especially in settlements on leeward coasts too arid even for dryland kalo. The hardiest varieties of ʻuala will tolerate habitats with less than thirty inches of rainfall annually, and the plant will grow successfully in almost any kind of earth except a sticky, clay-like soil." (Abbott 1992 p. 28)`, `"ʻUala were usually planted on the first to sixth days after the new moon---the nights designated as Hilo and Hoaka, plus the four Kū moons---and at full moon. The Hawaiians followed their planting calendar closely in growing ʻuala because this crop was substantially, if not wholly, dependent on rain; irrigation was not feasible in the areas where it was planted." (Abbott 1992 p. 30)`, `"Though ʻuala, like most plants, grow better in moist soil than in dry, they do not flourish in soil that is continually wet, and this fact was taken into account both in situating māla and in deciding when to plant. In dry localities, Hawaiians waited until a period of rain had started before planting ʻuala, while in wet areas, they waited until the rainy season appeared to be over." (Abbott 1992 p. 30)`, `"Though the tubers root very easily, in general practice the Hawaiians started new plants from slips (vine cuttings). Development of roots was sometimes promoted by first enclosing bundles of slips in damp tī leaves. Much of the work was done at night or before dawn, as Hawaiian planters believed that slips cut when the sun was up produced dry, stunted tubers and that slips planted when the sun was high formed plants with many leaves and few tubers. Two or three slips were placed in each mound, in holes six to eight inches deep made with an ʻōʻō, and the earth was pressed down around them. Mounds were then covered with a mulch to hold the moisure. In time, when growth appeared to be vigorous, the branches of each vine were twisted together around their own bases and covered with earth to induce each plant to produce larger tubers rather than more leaves. In the event of heavy rains, maturing or already mature vines were pulled back over the tops of the mounds to dry the foliage and to allow sun and wind to dry the soil beneath the vines in order to prevent mildew and rot." (Abbott 1992 p. 30-31)`, `"Two or three crops of ʻuala could be harvested from a single field each year if it were planted in the quick-growing varieties that mature in four to six months. This contrasts to one crop of kalo, which takes between nine and eighteen months to mature." (Abbott 1992 p. 31)`],
    interviewTitle: ``,
    interviewLines: [],
    oleloNoeau: [{ num: `50`, text: `Aia I luna o ʻUalakaʻa (He is up on ʻUalakaʻa). Said of one who, like a rolling potato, has nothing to hold fast to. The hill was said to have been named for a sweet potato that broke loose from its vine on a field above and rolled down to a field below in Mānoa.` }, { num: `329`, text: `E lawe i ke ō, he hinana ka iʻa kuhi lima (Take vegetable food; the hinana is a fish that can be caught in the hand). A suggestion to take taro, poi, potato, or breadfruit along on the journey and not worry about meats, which can be found along the way. First uttered by Pele in a chant about the winds of Kauaʻi.` }, { num: `570`, text: `He Hawaiʻi ʻuwala Kahiki (An Irish-potato Hawaiian). A term of derision applied to a native Hawaiian who apes the ways of the whites instead of appreciating the culture of his own people. Also said to one who is absolutely ignorant of his own culture.` }, { num: `946`, text: `He ʻuala ka ʻai hoʻōla koke i ka wi (The sweet potato is the food that ends famine quickly). The sweet potato is a plant that matures in a few months.` }, { num: `1444`, text: `Kālina ka pono, ʻaʻohe hua o ka puʻe, aia ka hua i ka lālā (The potato hill is bare of tubers for the plant no longer bears; it is the vines that are now bearing). The mother is no longer bearing, but her children are.` }, { num: `1483`, text: `Kamipulu Kawaihae (Damned fool of Kawaihae). Said of Kawaihae natives. Some natives of Kawaihae, Hawaiʻi, once sold sweet potatoes to the captain of a ship. He discovered some sticks placed at the bottom of the barrel for filler and called the men damned fools.` }, { num: `2123`, text: `Māla ʻuwala (Potato patch). Said in annoyance by an oldster when another Hawaiian asks in English, "What's the matter?" "Matter" sounds like "māla," and the retort "Māla ʻuwala" squelches any more questioning.` }, { num: `2165`, text: `Moʻa nopu o ke kau (Summer's first parched product). The first sweet potato of the summer or the first from one's field.` }, { num: `2291`, text: `Na puʻe ʻuwala ʻīnaʻi o ke ala loa (The sweet-potato mounds that provide for a long journey). Said of a patch of sweet potatoes whose crops are reserved for a voyage or journey.` }, { num: `2481`, text: `Ola i ka ʻōhulu (There is subsistence in the sprouting tubers). Said when there is a poor growth of sweet potatoes during an excessively warm summer. The broken pieces of potato sprouting among the weeds produces the few potatoes that feed the farmer and his family until a new crop is started.` }, { num: `2491`, text: `Ola no ka mahiʻai i kāhi kūʻōʻō (A farmer can subsist on small, broken potatoes). As long as there are potatoes, even small or broken ones, a farmer gets along.` }, { num: `2538`, text: `ʻŌpū palula (Stomach full of sweet-potato greens). Said of an ignorant person who can only grow sweet potatoes.` }, { num: `2762`, text: `Pūpū wahi kūʻōʻō ka mahiʻai o uka, ola no ia kini he mahiʻai na ka ʻōiwi (When the upland farmer gathers small, broken sweet potatoes there is life for many, though he only farms for himself). A farmer shares with beach dwellers.` }, { num: `2810`, text: `ʻUala liʻiliʻi o Kalepolepo (Small potatoes from Kalepolepo). Said of a stupid person.` }, { num: `2811`, text: `ʻUala neʻeneʻe o Kohala (Neʻeneʻe potato of Kohala). A person who hangs around constantly. Neʻeneʻe, a variety of sweet potato, also means "to move up closer."` }],
  },
  {
    id: 'ulu',
    name: 'ʻUlu',
    scientific: 'Artocarpus altilis',
    accent: '#5a7d3a',
    otherNames: `Breadfruit, Mei, Kamansi`,
    uses: `Canoe plant, food, wood.`,
    kinolau: `Kū`,
    profile: `Comes in both seedless and seeded varieties, can be grown on steep land where taro cannot, fruits range from .5lbs. to over 10 lbs., contains thiamine, high in starch, can be fermented or made into flour for preservation, high in vitamin B/C, reaches maturity after 5-7 years, mature trees can produce hundreds of fruits annually.`,
    vocab: [],
    other: [],
    planting: `Mostly propagated via adventitious aerial root propagation or air-layering. Certain varieties can be grown from seeds. Triploid varieties such as Maoli are seedless, while diploid varieties such as Otea, Fiti, and Maʻafala can reproduce sexually. Planting moons are Hilo, Kū Lua, Hua, Hoku, Māhealani, Kāne, Lono, and Mauli.`,
    mythologyRefs: [`*The Gift of Kū* (Hawaiʻi Island Legends, pp.168)`, `*The Girl Who Gave Breadfruit* (Hawaiʻi Island Legends, pp.24-28)`, `*Legend of Kahaʻi* (Hawaiian Mythology, pp.97-98)`],
    textExcerpts: [`"In some islands of Polynesia, such as Tahiti and the Marquesas, breadfruit is the primary food source. This is more due to geography than anything else. On the steep mountain slopes of the more-eroded Marquesan islands, breadfruit could be cultivated, while taro could not. In Hawaiʻi, the gentle, fertile slopes of the younger islands were perfect for growing taro, and breadfruit was therefore a less important crop. Still, there were huge lowland groves of ʻulu trees in Hawaiʻi. Among the first things mentioned by Archibald Menzies, the first Western botanist on Hawaiʻi Island, were the breadfruit plantations, which he stated were perfectly manicured. One fruit can weigh up to seven pounds and is high in starch as well as vitamins B and C." (Lincoln 2009 p. 95)`, `"Eaten mature but firm, the fruit has a mild, slightly sweet flavor that can be likened to varieties of ʻuala. A single mature fruit, weighing between two and seven pounds, is large enough to feed several people." (Abbott 1992 p. 35)`, `"Legend traces the origin of ʻulu to the god Kū, who turned himself into an ʻulu tree during a period of starvation in order that his wife and children could live." (Abbott 1992 p. 35)`, `"A member of the Moraceae or fig family, ʻulu grows to be a tall tree, frequently with a single, relatively straight trunk... ʻUlu leaves are large, deeply lobed or cleft, and have such a rough surface that dry leaves can serve as a kind of natural sandpaper. Trees bear separate heads of male and female flowers, the male consisting of numerous stamens around a club-shaped structure ten to twelve centimeters (four to five inches) long and the female having hundreds of flowers fused around a solid core that constitutes a fruit. The spherical fruit may grow as large as twenty-five centimeters (ten inches) in diameter, and in Hawaiʻi most of them ripen between May and October. The only variety of ʻulu grown in Hawaiʻi prior to Western contact was seedless, so propagation was necessarily vegetative... they are raised from erect root shoots, today generally known as 'suckers,' which sprout from the underground roots, and roots are sometimes cut in order to stimulate the production of root shoots. Once they sprout, the shoots must be carefully separated from the parent tree and replanted in an open area large enough to accommodate the tree's growth. New trees begin to bear fruit in three to five years and continue producing fruit for more than thirty-five years." (Abbott 1992 p. 36)`],
    interviewTitle: `From interview with Auntie LaVerne Lucero`,
    interviewLines: [`"I looked deeper into the intentions of my grandparents with the planting of the Hawaiian breadfruit variety that was planted on the existing property by both grandparents on opposite sides of the homestead property. As I reflected on the words of my grandfather, 'the ʻulu is your staple'... no further explanation---it was for me to go figure it out and try to understand grandpa's thinking or intentions. I've estimated the trees to be well over 80 years old and thriving. It not only feeds my neighbors but outsiders of Pacific Island descent as well as they so express appreciation for its sweet-tasting flavor. Monitoring the breadfruit yields year-to-year and weighing each fruit has brought me to see the fruitage of what just two generations can bring. I've estimated that one season of fruit by one tree alone can feed multiple families not just in the hundredfold, but by the thousands for an average household of four persons and a large community such as Keaukaha... A point of significance is that I believe our ʻulu trees originate from Hoʻokena on the west side simply because that is where my grandfather was raised before eventually relocating to Keaukaha together with grandma and raised their six children."`, `"As the ʻulu started to become revitalized and seeing the potential it has in food sustainability, my vision would manifest itself and I found it interesting how things would fall into place if I was alert to recognize its full potential and work along with the opportunities as they presented themselves."`, `"I learned that the ʻulu can never be wasted in any phase of its ripening. There is so much appreciation for the breadfruit these days than ever before and I believe it will only get better with the passing of time. My wishful thinking is that every home should have an ʻulu tree growing for their families."`, `"As I mentioned earlier the saying, 'We plant today for the next generations.' So if you plant a breadfruit today, remember you're planting for two generations later. My grandparents are wise for having 'put down roots' for their family. I want to ensure that each time I share our stories of the breadfruit that credit not only goes back to our creator but to those of our grandparents that laid those roots down."`, `"In referencing the ʻulu, observing and keeping track of your environmental climate and conditions is a good thing. My experience, as previously mentioned: the west sunlight during fall season may not give a tree bearing fruit full exposure, only maybe the southwest sunlight. If high winds during hurricane season, the flowering phase will lose its buds prematurely. If not enough rains or a very drought season, fruit will not fully develop or even prematurely drop to the ground. These seasonal conditions may not make for a good crop season. Just the same, whatever fruit it does produce is plenty enough to feed a family size of four people. Other destroyers of the ʻulu tree can be feral pigs that roam freely onto an unfenced property and will rub against the bark of your trees. You can also have viruses that may affect the outer skin of the breadfruit, making its appearance, perhaps if you're in the market, not so appealing for sales."`],
    oleloNoeau: [{ num: `213`, text: `ʻAʻohe ulu e loaʻa i ka pōkole o ka lou (No breadfruit can be reached when the picking stick is too short). There is no success without preparation.` }, { num: `329`, text: `E lawe i ke ō, he hinana ka iʻa kuhi lima (Take vegetable food; the hinana is a fish that can be caught in the hand). A suggestion to take taro, poi, potato, or breadfruit along on the journey and not worry about meats, which can be found along the way. First uttered by Pele in a chant about the winds of Kauaʻi.` }, { num: `959`, text: `He ʻulu ʻaʻai ʻole; he hāʻule wale i ka makani (It is a breadfruit that does not hold to the tree; it falls easily with the wind). Said of a person whose loyalty is doubtful---he can be swayed to desert his chief.` }, { num: `1117`, text: `Huaʻi ka ʻulu o Lele i ka makani Kona (The breadfruit of Lele is exposed by the Kona wind). Hidden matters are exposed in times of anger. When the Kona wind blows, the leaves of the trees are blown off to expose the fruit.` }, { num: `1201`, text: `I ke alo no ka ʻulu a hala (The breadfruit was just in front and it was missed).` }, { num: `1270`, text: `Ka ʻai kīʻoʻe lāʻau (The food reached for with a stick). Said of the breadfruit, which grows high on the tree.` }, { num: `1273`, text: `Ka ʻai nānā i luna (The food that requires looking up to). Said of breadfruit, which grows on the tree, in contrast with taro, sweet potato, and yam, which grow underground.` }, { num: `1627`, text: `Ka ʻulu loaʻa ʻole i ka lou ʻia (The breadfruit that even a pole cannot reach). Said of a person of very high rank.` }, { num: `1895`, text: `Ku ka ule, heʻe ka laho (The penis stands, the scrotum sags). This expression is not meant to be vulgar. When the ule or pōule (breadfruit blossom) appears, it is the sign of the fruiting season. The young breadfruit first appears upright, and as the fruit grows larger its stem bends so that it hangs downward.` }, { num: `1942`, text: `Lālau aku ʻoe i ka ʻulu i ka wekiu, i ke alo no ka ʻulu, a hala (You reach for the breadfruit away at the top and miss the one in front of you). Sometimes one who reaches afar misses an opportunity that is right before him. Once Kalākaua promised to give a better position to Kamaʻiopili of Maui, but then forgot his promise. One day, while playing billiards with the king, Kamaʻiopili purposely played very badly and exclaimed, "I ke alo no ka ʻulu, a hala," whenever he missed the cue ball (ʻulu). This puzzled the king, and when the game was over, he asked a man who knew all the old sayings what Kamaʻiopili had meant. The king was told that Kamaʻiopili was reminding him that others had been rewarded with good positions, but that the man right in front of him, Kamaʻiopili, had been forgotten.` }, { num: `2078`, text: `Mai lou i ka ʻulu i luna lilo, o lou hewa i ka ʻaʻai ʻole; eia no ka ʻulu i ke alo (Do not hook the breadfruit away up above lest you hook an imperfect one; take the one in front of you). Why reach afar for a mate? Choose one from among your own acquaintances.` }, { num: `2080`, text: `Mai nānā i ka ʻulu o waho, ʻaʻohe ia nau; e nānā no i ka ʻulu i ke alo, nau ia (Never mind looking for the breadfruit away out, that is not for you; look at the breadfruit in front of you, that is yours). Be satisfied with what you have.` }, { num: `2186`, text: `Moku i ka ʻohe a Kahaʻi (Cut off by the bamboo knife of Kahaʻi). Said of any complete severing. Kahaʻi was a chief who traveled afar. His is credited with introducing the first breadfruit plant to the islands.` }, { num: `2271`, text: `Nānā no a ka ʻulu i pakī kēpau (Look for the gummy breadfruit). Advice to a young girl---Look for a man who has substance, like gummy breadfruit, which is a sign of maturity.` }, { num: `2297`, text: `Na ʻulu hua i ka hāpapa (The breadfruit that bears on the ground). Breadfruit trees of Niʻihau were grown in sinkholes. The trunks were not visible, and the branches seemed to spread along the ground. These trees are famed in chants of Niʻihau.` }, { num: `2446`, text: `O ka ʻulu o lalo he loaʻa i ka pinana, o ka ʻulu o luna loa he loaʻa i ka lou (A breadfruit that is low can be reached by climbing, but a breadfruit high above requires a stick to reach it). A mate of low station is easy to find, but one of higher rank is less easily acquired.` }, { num: `2584`, text: `Pakī kēpau, oʻo ka ʻulu (When the gum appears on the skin, the breadfruit is matured). An observation. Also said when a young person begins to think seriously of gaining a livelihood---he is maturing.` }, { num: `2753`, text: `Pupuhi ka ʻulu o Keʻei; ua koe ka ʻaʻaiole (The breadfruit of Keʻei are gone; only those blown down by the wind are left). Said when something mysteriously vanishes. A konohiki of Keʻei in Kona, Hawaiʻi, was placed in charge of a fine breadfruit grove. In spite of his watchfulness, the fruit were stolen as soon as they matured. Secretly he asked all of his relatives to help him watch for the culprit. However, some were related to the thief as well, who learned about the watch and evaded capture. Long after, a slip of the tongue revealed the thief.` }, { num: `2871`, text: `ʻUlu pilo (Stinking breadfruit). A term of contempt for the kauwā of Puna, Hawaiʻi, comparing them to rotted breadfruit.` }],
  },
  {
    id: 'awa',
    name: 'ʻAwa',
    scientific: 'Piper methysticum',
    accent: '#6b5b95',
    otherNames: `Kava, Kavakava, Kawa, Kawakawa, Yangona`,
    uses: `Roots pounded with water for medicine, religious/spiritual ceremony, recreation, ease muscles, sleep aid.`,
    kinolau: `Kāne`,
    profile: `Narcotic properties (heightened senses, dulled pain, etc.) due to kavalactones. Only roots should be used for consumption; stems and leaves can contain dangerous compounds. 3-5 years to maturity. Tongan/Samoan/Fijian varieties are harder on the kidneys and are used mostly for ceremonial purposes. Hawaiian varieties are known as "noble" varieties, and can be used more regularly with fewer negative effects on the body. Hawaiian varieties have had their origins (genetically) traced to the Marquesas, with various legends describing their origins and introduction to Hawaiʻi. Varieties with longer internodes tend to prefer a sub-canopy environment with more shade. Bushier varieties with shorter internodes tend to prefer more sunlight, and are more wind-resistant. Varieties are traditionally identified and categorized based on physical characteristics (color, spotting, and length of internodes).`,
    vocab: [`ʻAʻa (aerial roots)`, `ʻAka (nodes)`],
    other: [`The Panaʻewa variety was discovered and named by Genesis Lee Loy (Grandfather of Makaʻala Rawlins). The Panaʻewa variety has short internodes. Internodes are green, with a bit of dark purple just above each node, and very few lenticels (spots). The Papa ʻEleʻele variety has short internodes. It is dark purple/green above the nodes which fade into a dark green/brown at the internodes. The Hanakāpīʻai variety has long green internodes with many purple lenticels (spots) and purple nodes. The Hiwa variety is the darkest of the Hawaiian varieties. It has long internodes with few lenticels (spots) and is easily identified by its uniform dark purple branches.`, `An online copy of *Hawaiian ʻAwa: Views of an Ethnobotanical Treasure* can be found at <https://www.awadevelopment.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/01/awabook-web-part-1-of-2.pdf>`, `and <https://www.awadevelopment.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/01/awabook-web-part-2-of-2.pdf>`],
    planting: `Propagated by cuttings of mature plants`,
    mythologyRefs: [`*ʻAwa---He Moʻolelo Kaʻao* (Hawaiian ʻAwa: Views of an Ethnobotanical Treasure, pp. 11-14)`],
    textExcerpts: [`"*ʻAwa* was an important medicine and was also used extensively in religious and spiritual ceremonies. The plant takes about three years to mature, at which point the root is harvested, pounded with water, and strained into a strong drink. This drink exhibits narcotic properties, including heightened senses and dulled pain. In fact, the scientific name literally means 'intoxicating pepper.' ʻAwa was frequently drunk by the commoners, primarily to ease weary muscles and aid in sleep. The *aliʻi*, or chiefs, also drank *ʻawa* for recreational purposes and would consume large quantities as a show of power. The plant was also used extensively by a third class of Hawaiians, the *kāhuna*... Depending on their specialty, *kāhuna* would use ʻawa in varying quantities. For example, one might consume large amounts of strong ʻawa to evoke spiritual power for divination, or take small amounts of ʻawa to hone the senses and prepare for battle." (Lincoln 2009 p. 81)`, `"A member of the black pepper family, ʻawa usually takes the form of a large, rounded shrub with long branches. The branches have nodes (joints), and markings on the internodes---the sections between the joints---furnish some of the characteristics by which ʻawa varieties can be distinguished... The heart-shaped leaves are somewhat velvety when young. Male and female flowers are borne on separate plants, and in Hawaiʻi seeds are rarely set... Stem sections are cut and laid along the ground, covered lightly with soil, and allowed to root." (Abbott 1992 p. 43)`, `"In addition to its normal use, in which it is served as a relaxant and soporific, ʻawa was blended with various other substances for healing purposes. An extract of ʻawa root was used in remedies to relieve difficulty in urinating, menstrual irregularities, and congestion in the respiratory tract." (Abbott 1992 p. 101)`, `"A few plants and plant products received regular ceremonial use... None had a more conspicuous role in religious ceremonies than ʻawa. A portion of dried root or a cup of the drink was frequently included in offerings to the gods, and priests often drank ʻawa at the end of a ceremony. It was offered to the gods at the onset of war, in consecrating a male child, in the ceremonial initiation of girls trained in hula and chanting, in rituals directed by kahuna lapaʻau to promote healing, and in seances in which a god or goddess spoke through a medium. An offering of ʻawa was a requirement at certain occasions such as felling a koa tree to make a canoe." (Abbott 1992 p. 115)`, `"['Awa] has become prominent in alternative medicine for its ability to reduce anxiety, soothe sore muscles, and induce re laxation, calm, and sleep, without being addictive or impairing the user's judgment." (Johnston and Rogers 2006 p.1)`, `"There are several native traditions regarding the origin of 'awa in Hawai'i. Perhaps the most significant narratives describe 'awa as having been brought to Hawai'i from Kahiki (the ancestral homelands) by the akua (gods) Kāne and Kanaloa. These two akua...planted 'awa at various localities throughout the islands. In places where no water could be found with which to prepare the 'awa, Kāne even caused water to appear, thus forming many springs and streams in the islands." (Johnston and Rogers 2006 p. 8)`, `"Kavalactones do not induce sleep like true sedatives and do not inhibit the sensitivity of sensory nerves, nor do they reduce motor activity, but rather act as muscle relaxants and enhancers of deep sleep. Unlike sedatives, the kavalactones appear to work on the limbic system of the brain." (Johnston and Rogers 2006 p. 17)`, `"The major active ingredients in kava are known as kavalactones and are concentrated in the rootstock and roots rather than in the aerial parts (stems and leaves), which contain kavalactones in smaller amounts. Interestingly, leaves and peelings from stems contain another class of compounds called alkaloids, notably pipermethystine, and these alkaloids are potentially dangerous to ingest." (Johnston and Rogers 2006 p. 19)`, `"The traits distinguishing the cultivars can be difficult to perceive. In describing the plant, Margaret Titcomb reported that 'The stems are jointed, the spaces between the joints sometimes determining the native name of the species, as do also the intensity of the green of the leaves, the colour of the stems and the quality of the root' (Titcomb 1948, 109). This is not so different from the way we tell the cultivars apart in Hawai'i today. We rely more on the characteristics of the stalks than on the leaves. The leaf characteristics of Hawai'i's cultivars are quite similar, unlike in Vanuatu where there is a greater amount of variation in their 'awa." (Johnston and Rogers 2006 p. 30)`, `"Kavain is the predominant kavalactone in all Hawaiian varieties, and it lays the foundation for the excellent drinking experience to be had with our varieties. Kavain creates the feeling of well-being or mild euphoria that is the hallmark of the 'awa experience. It also acts quickly--within ten minutes or so." (Johnston and Rogers 2006 p. 31)`],
    interviewTitle: `From interview with Makaʻala Rawlins`,
    interviewLines: [`MR: Our family also was able to grow ʻawa, because my grandfather, in the 90s, he found ʻawa growing wild on one of our uncle's lots, and he propagated that. And so ever since then, we've been also growing ʻawa as well. My grandfather, Genesis Lee Loy, and Jerry Konanui, and Edward Johnson started this Association for Hawaiian ʻAwa back in the 90s, as well.`, `TL: So the ʻawa that he found, is that the Panaʻewa-marked one that's in the nursery?`, `MR: Yes, it's the Panaʻewa variety that we still continue to grow today. And, yeah, whenever I look at it, I think of him. And so those kinds of things are a good reminder for myself as to why we continue to do these things. And trying to keep it going so hopefully, the next generation, if they want to take it over, they'll have something to step into.`, `TL: What are the characteristics of the Panaʻewa variety?`, `MR: Well, the Panaʻewa variety of ʻawa is more of a short internode ʻawa. So usually, you have ʻawa that has long nodes, or long spaces between nodes, and this variety has a shorter node. And you can tell it because it's mostly green, and then when you get to the nodes, it becomes a little bit like dark purplish. And those are kind of indicators of that variety of ʻawa. They named it "Panaʻewa" because my grandfather found it in Panaʻewa, and it wasn't necessarily lining up to some of the other ʻawa varieties that we currently have. Because for ʻawa, especially Hawaiian ʻawa, we trace some of the genetics back to the Marquesas. But ʻawa in general, when you see different varieties of ʻawa, they're usually mutations. They have, I believe it's called a "somatic mutation." So the they will oftentimes have different nodes that pop up that look different than the current variety that you're growing. And so, what people do-- what people have done in the past, is take that different offshoot because it's a variation, and then cultivate it. And then usually keep that genetic variety going afterwards.`, `TL: Is ʻawa something that has seasonality at all? Or not really?`, `MR: Not really. Summertime they tend to grow well. And it's one of those things-- ʻawa is one of those things that you leave in the ground for about two and a half years to five years. And then you harvest the root ball. The aerial root is the one that gives you the kavalactone-- the high kavalactone content, so that's what you want is a big aerial root, the runners.`, `TL: It takes about two to five years for them to start putting those out?`, `MR: Yeah. Uncle Jerry Konanui and my grandfather guys would say, "The difference between a two-and-a-half-year ʻawa plant and a five-year-old ʻawa plant is: one you have to use an ʻōʻō, and the other you have to use an excavator." [Laughing] Meaning that they get big, right? And not necessarily the kavalactone potency increases or decreases with age, but it's just a bigger plant that you have to deal with.`, `TL: So, it's not necessarily more potent if it's older?`, `MR: No. I believe it's about two and a half years when it reaches its maximum potency.`, `TL: And then it just stays there?`, `MR: Yeah. And then it just gets bigger, right? The aerial root gets bigger, so it's more you have to dig out. So, that's kind of-- they jokingly said that. I remember that, I was like, huh? And then when I harvested ʻawa, it was like, oh, okay, I get it now. [Laughing]`, `TL: Is there a best time to plant or to propagate that?`, `MR: You know, I kind of like to propagate it outside of summertime, as we're going into more like fall, towards that time. And for me, it's mostly because, you know, in fall you're stuck the rainy season here in Hawaiʻi, so you won't have to water as much. That's kind of my thinking on it. Although, it's a pretty hearty plant and you can probably propagate it throughout the year, just taking it from mature plants, taking your nodes from more mature plants.`],
    oleloNoeau: [{ num: `246`, text: `ʻAwa kau lāʻau o Puna (Tree-growing ʻawa of Puna). Tree-grown ʻawa of Puna was famous for its potency. It was believed that birds carried pieces of ʻawa up into the trees where it would grow.` }, { num: `275`, text: `E hānai ʻawa a ikaika ka makani (Feed with ʻawa so that the spirit may gain strength). One offers ʻawa and prayers to the dead so that their spirits may grow strong and be a source of help to the family.` }, { num: `1281`, text: `Ka ʻawa lena o Kaliʻu (The yellowed ʻawa of Kaliʻu). Refers to Kaliʻu, Kilohana, Kauaʻi. People noticed drunken rats in the forest and discovered some very potent ʻawa there. There is a Kaliʻu in Puna, Hawaiʻi, where good ʻawa is also grown.` }, { num: `1701`, text: `Ke iho mai nei ko luna (Those above are descending). A fog is beginning to settle. Said by one who is beginning to feel the effects of the ʻawa he has drunk.` }, { num: `2744`, text: `Puna, ʻāina ʻawa lau o ka manu (Puna, land of the leafed ʻawa planted by the birds).` }],
  },
];
